      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After being platted, Park Rapids residents founded a Ladies Library that opened a free public library in the fire station, later relocating it to a former bank in 1994.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Rapids%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2022, the Anishinaabe nonprofit Akiing purchased the former library building and renamed it the Giiwedinong Treaty Rights and Culture Museum, part of a Land Back effort.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Rapids%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The land that is now Park Rapids was originally inhabited by Ojibwe people, who still maintain a significant presence in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Rapids%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Park Rapids sits along the Fish Hook River and is surrounded by three lakes—Fish Hook, Long, and Potato—providing ample fishing opportunities.",
